INT. AUCTION HOUSE VIEWING ROOM - NIGHT

Velvet ropes. Glass cases of antique clocks, each ticking out of step. A single chandelier left on.

NADIA FAIRWEATHER, 40s, in a grey cashmere coat, circles a case holding a mantel clock. GRAHAM OSTRANDER, 50s, the auctioneer, stands at the door with a ring of keys. LUCINDA PASCOE, 30s, in a catalog-girl blazer, arranges lot cards that are already straight. EMEKA ROWE, 50s, a security guard, stands by the fire exit with his arms folded.

NADIA
Lot forty-one has a new ticket.

GRAHAM
We reprinted them.

NADIA
Reprinted.

Graham jingles his keys. Lucinda straightens a card that doesn't need it. Emeka looks at the ceiling.

NADIA (CONT'D)
(to the room)
Does anyone remember what the old ticket said?

Nobody answers. A clock chimes the quarter hour. Another follows, late.

LUCINDA
It said, um, "attributed to."

NADIA
And the new one?

LUCINDA
"Signed."

Nadia opens her handbag. She takes out a small brass loupe and bends over the case. She studies the dial for a long moment. When she straightens, her face gives away nothing.

NADIA
(low, to herself)
Twelve years.

GRAHAM
Nadia, it's late. We can discuss it in the morning.

NADIA
I'd rather discuss it now, with Emeka present.

Emeka unfolds his arms. Graham's keys stop jingling.

EMEKA
Ma'am.

NADIA
Emeka, what time did the courier leave?

EMEKA
Six forty.

NADIA
And who signed him out?

Lucinda lets a lot card fall. She doesn't pick it up.

GRAHAM
That's enough.

NADIA
Is it?

She slips the loupe back into the handbag and snaps it shut. The sound carries. She walks the length of the room, her heels clicking in time with no clock, and stops at the door beside Graham.

NADIA (CONT'D)
I'll be bidding on forty-one tomorrow. I suggest the ticket stay as it is.

GRAHAM
Of course.

NADIA
Good night, everyone.

She goes out. The door closes with a soft click. For a long while, no one moves.

LUCINDA
(barely audible)
She knows.

EMEKA
Keep your voice down.

GRAHAM
(to himself)
Twelve years. Twelve years.
